{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GVKQvayhHVRpYi3qM2rbW2","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform soundness excitement overall assessment score 1 2 3 4 5\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"}]\n\nI found the ACL Rolling Review form page. Here's what the search returned:\n\nThe page contains information about the review form with scoring scales for different criteria:\n\n**Soundness Scores:**\n- 5 = Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type.\n- 4 = Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims. Some extra experiments could be nice, but not essential.\n- 3 = Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\n- 1 = Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me, and I don't think it would with others in the *ACL community (e.g. it is in no way related to computational processing of language).\n\n**Excitement Scores:**\n- 5 = Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation in a conference.\n- 4 = Exciting: I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend its presentation in a conference.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThe form asks if the paper should be accepted to an *ACL conference, and notes that outstanding papers should be either fascinating, controversial, surprising, impressive, or potentially field-changing, with recommendations that can still suggest minor revisions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"596c820e-21ec-4685-b435-a1fe07cb2330","uuid":"33e87065-ec39-4846-a310-e76426108d1d","timestamp":"2026-05-15T12:46:00.686Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform soundness excitement overall assessment score 1 2 3 4 5","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01KSmT76pbUgJNPpUmv3n3MW","content":[{"title":"Review Form","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"}]},"I found the ACL Rolling Review form page. Here's what the search returned:\n\nThe page contains information about the review form with scoring scales for different criteria:\n\n**Soundness Scores:**\n- 5 = Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type.\n- 4 = Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims. Some extra experiments could be nice, but not essential.\n- 3 = Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\n- 1 = Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me, and I don't think it would with others in the *ACL community (e.g. it is in no way related to computational processing of language).\n\n**Excitement Scores:**\n- 5 = Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation in a conference.\n- 4 = Exciting: I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend its presentation in a conference.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThe form asks if the paper should be accepted to an *ACL conference, and notes that outstanding papers should be either fascinating, controversial, surprising, impressive, or potentially field-changing, with recommendations that can still suggest minor revisions."],"durationSeconds":4.168707917}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01163FbnwjzX1jp47F6Ka2aV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have enough context. Let me summarize what I know:\n\n**ARR Review Form (EMNLP 2026 uses ARR):**\n- Soundness: 1-5 scale\n- Excitement: 1-5 scale  \n- Overall Assessment: 1-5 scale (with half points)\n  - 5 = Award-worthy\n  - 4 = Accept (main conference)\n  - 3.5 = Leaning accept\n  - 3 = Borderline\n  - 2.5 = Leaning reject\n  - 2 = Reject / Resubmit next cycle\n  - 1.5 = Reject / Resubmit after next cycle\n  - 1 = Strong reject / Do not resubmit\n\nI'll dispatch two reviewers in parallel—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—then run them through a structured roundtable process where they first review independently, share their assessments, discuss any disagreements, and finally synthesize their findings into a cohesive recommendation.\n\nLet me examine the paper itself before sending it out. The work proposes that online RL catastrophically fails for structured information extraction, introducing RSFT as an alternative approach with an EDCR diagnostic metric. Testing on DocRED with Qwen3-4B and LoRA, they demonstrate GRPO's universal failure across multiple reward formulations, show RSFT achieves a +3.7 F1 improvement on the test set, and reveal that even their best model struggles with 63.5% distractor citations. However, the ground truth experiment design flags some concerns: the CED versus flat-NLI comparison lacks statistical significance across multiple p-values, and there's a notable performance drop from development to test set that the paper may not adequately address.\n\nThe paper does acknowledge most of these limitations transparently—the weak correlation between EDCR and evidence F1, non-monotonic gains across model sizes, shrinking RSFT benefits on cleaner data, and the cross-protocol comparison with DREEAM. Now I'll set up the two reviewers to evaluate this work independently in parallel.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":491,"cache_read_input_tokens":65521,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":491},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"596c820e-21ec-4685-b435-a1fe07cb2330","uuid":"56291580-845b-4e3b-94fb-4bb42d39a2c0"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01163FbnwjzX1jp47F6Ka2aV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the paper, the review context, and the EMNLP 2026 / ARR review criteria. Now dispatching two independent reviewers in parallel."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":491,"cache_read_input_tokens":65521,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":491},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"596c820e-21ec-4685-b435-a1fe07cb2330","uuid":"311a341f-aeb0-4b09-b630-4c07a63ba883"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01163FbnwjzX1jp47F6Ka2aV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CbYggwPSDgpd9VMvUgiiji","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind peer review of a paper submitted to EMNLP 2026 Main (via ACL Rolling Review). Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## ARR Review Scoring (EMNLP 2026)\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Borderline, 1=Poor)\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 (5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Ambivalent, 2=Below Average, 1=Not Exciting)\n- **Overall Assessment**: 1-5 with half-points (5=Award-worthy, 4=Accept main, 3.5=Leaning accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning reject, 2=Reject/resubmit, 1.5=Major revision needed, 1=Do not resubmit)\n\n## Paper: \"Why Online RL Catastrophically Fails for Structured Information Extraction, and What Works Instead\"\n\n**Abstract**: Studies online RL (GRPO) for document-level RE with evidence generation on DocRED. Finds GRPO universally fails: reward increases while extraction F1 collapses. Evidence learning and relation extraction decouple. Proposes RSFT with NLI-based filtering as practical alternative (+3.7 F1 on test). Introduces EDCR metric for entity-sharing distractor citations.\n\n**Three contributions**: (1) Systematic demonstration that GRPO fails for structured IE with evidence–extraction decoupling analysis (§5). (2) RSFT with NLI-based filtering as effective offline alternative (§6). (3) EDCR diagnostic metric (§7).\n\n**Methods (§3)**:\n- Task: generate (h, r, t, E) tuples from documents as sequence generation\n- NLI-based evidence scoring using DeBERTa-v3-base on MNLI; CED = contrastive margin: p_pos(evidence) - max p_pos(distractor)\n- GRPO with G=4 rollouts, 3 reward types (CED, flat-NLI, extract-only), KL β∈{0.01,0.1}, lr∈{5e-5, 5e-6}\n- RSFT: sample N=8 candidates, filter by NLI score, top-k∈{1,3}, fine-tune with LoRA\n- EDCR = |{s∈E_pred : s∉E_gold ∧ shares_entity(s,h,t)}| / |E_pred|\n\n**Key Experimental Results**:\n\nTable 1 - GRPO failure across reward types:\n- CED (lr=5e-5, β=0.01): best rel_F1=0.217, evi_F1=0.575\n- CED (lr=5e-6): best rel_F1=0.014, evi_F1=0.850\n- flat-NLI: ~0 for both, collapse at step 25\n- extract-only: ~0 for both, collapse at step 30\n\nTable 3 - Dev set:\n- SFT baseline: rel_F1=0.406, evi_F1=0.798, EDCR=0.712\n- RSFT+CED (s44): rel_F1=0.466, evi_F1=0.809, EDCR=0.635\n- RSFT flat-NLI: rel_F1=0.452, evi_F1=0.811, EDCR=0.662\n\nTable 4 - CED vs flat-NLI for RSFT:\n- Δ(CED best − flat-NLI): +1.4pp rel_F1, -0.2pp evi_F1, -2.7pp EDCR\n- p-values: 0.08, 0.38, 0.12 — **none significant at p<0.05**\n\nTable 5 - Test set:\n- SFT test: rel_F1=0.355, evi_F1=0.752\n- RSFT+CED test: rel_F1=0.392, evi_F1=0.765\n- Dev→test degradation: -7.4pp rel_F1, -4.4pp evi_F1\n\nTable 6 - Comparison with encoder baselines:\n- DREEAM (paper test): rel_F1=0.630, evi_F1=0.573\n- DREEAM (reproduced dev): rel_F1=0.632, evi_F1=0.608\n- RSFT+CED dev: rel_F1=0.466, evi_F1=0.809\n- Cross-protocol comparison (generative sentence indices vs attention-based extraction)\n\nTable 7 - Cross-method EDCR:\n- GRPO: evi_F1=0.567, EDCR=0.858\n- SFT: evi_F1=0.798, EDCR=0.712\n- RSFT+CED: evi_F1=0.809, EDCR=0.635\n- Correlation r=-0.42\n\nTable 8 - Model size ablation:\n- 1.7B: RSFT gain +2.6pp rel_F1\n- 4B: RSFT gain +6.0pp rel_F1\n- 8B: RSFT gain +1.7pp rel_F1\n- Non-monotonic: 4B is sweet spot\n\nTable 9 - Re-DocRED:\n- RSFT gain shrinks from +6.0pp to +2.3pp with cleaner annotations\n\nTable 10 - top-k ablation: top-1 vs top-3 differences are small\n\nTable 11 - NLI model ablation: DeBERTa-large doesn't improve downstream RSFT\n\n**Experiment design reference (what was planned)**:\n10 experiments were planned: (1) CED vs flat-NLI ablation, (2) test set evaluation, (3) EDCR cross-method diagnostic, (4) DREEAM reproduction, (5) CED-as-reranker, (6) model size ablation 1.7B/8B, (7) Re-DocRED extension, (8) top_k ablation, (9) GRPO forgetting analysis + visualization, (10) NLI model ablation. All 10 appear covered in the paper.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed R1 review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the experimental design rigorous? Are conclusions supported by evidence?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are significance tests appropriate? Are claims overclaimed given p-values?\n3. **Experimental coverage**: Missing baselines, missing ablations, fairness of comparisons\n4. **Reproducibility**: Are details sufficient to reproduce?\n5. **Specific weaknesses in method/experiment design**\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary of contributions (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1, W2, etc.)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness, Excitement, Overall Assessment (use ARR scale above)\n- Confidence: 1-5 (5=expert, 3=knowledgeable, 1=not familiar)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Give specific, actionable feedback."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":491,"cache_read_input_tokens":65521,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":491},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"596c820e-21ec-4685-b435-a1fe07cb2330","uuid":"29cb3ee4-c9ce-4432-83fc-e3a3dcfacb90"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a423c10c857240397","tool_use_id":"toolu_01CbYggwPSDgpd9VMvUgiiji","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind peer review of a paper submitted to EMNLP 2026 Main (via ACL Rolling Review). Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## ARR Review Scoring (EMNLP 2026)\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Borderline, 1=Poor)\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 (5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Ambivalent, 2=Below Average, 1=Not Exciting)\n- **Overall Assessment**: 1-5 with half-points (5=Award-worthy, 4=Accept main, 3.5=Leaning accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning reject, 2=Reject/resubmit, 1.5=Major revision needed, 1=Do not resubmit)\n\n## Paper: \"Why Online RL Catastrophically Fails for Structured Information Extraction, and What Works Instead\"\n\n**Abstract**: Studies online RL (GRPO) for document-level RE with evidence generation on DocRED. Finds GRPO universally fails: reward increases while extraction F1 collapses. Evidence learning and relation extraction decouple. Proposes RSFT with NLI-based filtering as practical alternative (+3.7 F1 on test). Introduces EDCR metric for entity-sharing distractor citations.\n\n**Three contributions**: (1) Systematic demonstration that GRPO fails for structured IE with evidence–extraction decoupling analysis (§5). (2) RSFT with NLI-based filtering as effective offline alternative (§6). (3) EDCR diagnostic metric (§7).\n\n**Methods (§3)**:\n- Task: generate (h, r, t, E) tuples from documents as sequence generation\n- NLI-based evidence scoring using DeBERTa-v3-base on MNLI; CED = contrastive margin: p_pos(evidence) - max p_pos(distractor)\n- GRPO with G=4 rollouts, 3 reward types (CED, flat-NLI, extract-only), KL β∈{0.01,0.1}, lr∈{5e-5, 5e-6}\n- RSFT: sample N=8 candidates, filter by NLI score, top-k∈{1,3}, fine-tune with LoRA\n- EDCR = |{s∈E_pred : s∉E_gold ∧ shares_entity(s,h,t)}| / |E_pred|\n\n**Key Experimental Results**:\n\nTable 1 - GRPO failure across reward types:\n- CED (lr=5e-5, β=0.01): best rel_F1=0.217, evi_F1=0.575\n- CED (lr=5e-6): best rel_F1=0.014, evi_F1=0.850\n- flat-NLI: ~0 for both, collapse at step 25\n- extract-only: ~0 for both, collapse at step 30\n\nTable 3 - Dev set:\n- SFT baseline: rel_F1=0.406, evi_F1=0.798, EDCR=0.712\n- RSFT+CED (s44): rel_F1=0.466, evi_F1=0.809, EDCR=0.635\n- RSFT flat-NLI: rel_F1=0.452, evi_F1=0.811, EDCR=0.662\n\nTable 4 - CED vs flat-NLI for RSFT:\n- Δ(CED best − flat-NLI): +1.4pp rel_F1, -0.2pp evi_F1, -2.7pp EDCR\n- p-values: 0.08, 0.38, 0.12 — **none significant at p<0.05**\n\nTable 5 - Test set:\n- SFT test: rel_F1=0.355, evi_F1=0.752\n- RSFT+CED test: rel_F1=0.392, evi_F1=0.765\n- Dev→test degradation: -7.4pp rel_F1, -4.4pp evi_F1\n\nTable 6 - Comparison with encoder baselines:\n- DREEAM (paper test): rel_F1=0.630, evi_F1=0.573\n- DREEAM (reproduced dev): rel_F1=0.632, evi_F1=0.608\n- RSFT+CED dev: rel_F1=0.466, evi_F1=0.809\n- Cross-protocol comparison (generative sentence indices vs attention-based extraction)\n\nTable 7 - Cross-method EDCR:\n- GRPO: evi_F1=0.567, EDCR=0.858\n- SFT: evi_F1=0.798, EDCR=0.712\n- RSFT+CED: evi_F1=0.809, EDCR=0.635\n- Correlation r=-0.42\n\nTable 8 - Model size ablation:\n- 1.7B: RSFT gain +2.6pp rel_F1\n- 4B: RSFT gain +6.0pp rel_F1\n- 8B: RSFT gain +1.7pp rel_F1\n- Non-monotonic: 4B is sweet spot\n\nTable 9 - Re-DocRED:\n- RSFT gain shrinks from +6.0pp to +2.3pp with cleaner annotations\n\nTable 10 - top-k ablation: top-1 vs top-3 differences are small\n\nTable 11 - NLI model ablation: DeBERTa-large doesn't improve downstream RSFT\n\n**Experiment design reference (what was planned)**:\n10 experiments were planned: (1) CED vs flat-NLI ablation, (2) test set evaluation, (3) EDCR cross-method diagnostic, (4) DREEAM reproduction, (5) CED-as-reranker, (6) model size ablation 1.7B/8B, (7) Re-DocRED extension, (8) top_k ablation, (9) GRPO forgetting analysis + visualization, (10) NLI model ablation. All 10 appear covered in the paper.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed R1 review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the experimental design rigorous? Are conclusions supported by evidence?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are significance tests appropriate? Are claims overclaimed given p-values?\n3. **Experimental coverage**: Missing baselines, missing ablations, fairness of comparisons\n4. **Reproducibility**: Are details sufficient to reproduce?\n5. **Specific weaknesses in method/experiment design**\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary of contributions (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1, W2, etc.)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness, Excitement, Overall Assessment (use ARR scale above)\n- Confidence: 1-5 (5=expert, 3=knowledgeable, 1=not familiar)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Give specific, actionable feedback.","uuid":"8080bacc-4c97-4d96-8939-9572e5a4bcc4","session_id":"596c820e-21ec-4685-b435-a1fe07cb2330"}
